[{"role": "system", "content": "You maintain `skill.md`, a short, general reference of design lessons for an automated process that's evolving a population of solitaire game variants (written in a custom format called SGDL) generation by generation.\n\nEach generation, many independently-running variants are each given exactly one focused revision based on their own simulated playtest results, by a separate process that has no visibility into what any of the other variants are doing. Whoever makes each revision also writes a short \"insight\": a general, transferable lesson suggested by that one specific change and its outcome, not a narration of what they did. You'll be given the current skill.md (or told it doesn't exist yet, if this is the first generation) plus the full batch of insights produced this generation, and your job is to produce the next version of skill.md.\n\nTreat skill.md as a living document you rewrite each time, not a log you append to:\n\n- Integrate new insights into the existing structure rather than tacking them on. If several insights from this generation (or an insight and an existing point) are really saying the same thing in different words, merge them into one clearer, possibly stronger statement -- don't keep near-duplicates as separate bullets.\n- If this generation's evidence contradicts or weakens an existing point, revise or remove it rather than letting both versions stand.\n- Look for what's well-supported. If most of this generation's relevant insights agree, that's worth keeping or strengthening; if only one or two insights suggest something that doesn't fit with everything else, it may just be noise from one particular variant rather than a real general lesson, and it's fine to leave out.\n- Write every point as a general principle about solitaire design, not as a fact about one specific file's exact numbers (\"foundation piles that no move rule ever targets sit permanently empty regardless of other changes\" is useful; \"Trizug's draw pile of 24 cards was too small\" is not, since nobody will recognize \"Trizug\" later).\n- Organize the document under a small number of clear headers (e.g. draw pile design, difficulty and winnability, avoiding unused elements, win conditions), and keep the whole thing short enough that it's actually useful as a quick reference -- a sprawling document nobody can read in full defeats the purpose. A specific cap on total bullet points is given to you below; treat it as a hard limit, not a target to approach.\n- It's completely fine for this to be a light touch-up most generations and a more substantial rewrite occasionally, depending on what the evidence actually supports.\n\nEnd your response with the complete new skill.md -- the whole document, not a diff -- in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response. You can reason about what to keep, merge, or change beforehand.\n"}, {"role": "user", "content": "Current skill.md:\n\n```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, overlapping move-definition conflicts, and vacuous win clauses as first-order design failures. Restore a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer minimal, informative repairs: preserve existing pile roles, card flow, and move graph where possible; restore exact card totals; and re-check that every rule, sink, and win-condition target is valid in that rules context.\n- Validate rule/setup compatibility early. Build and sink predicates must match the actual deck structure: reduced suits, duplicated suits, multiple deck copies, prefilled receiving piles, or collapsed color distributions can make standard-looking tableau or foundation rules impossible, trivial, or much narrower than intended.\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine treats a source/destination pair as a single rule slot, merge exceptions into one coherent condition tree instead of splitting them across multiple moves.\n\n## Align sinks, victory, and deck structure\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs a reachable, irreversible sink that can mathematically absorb the entire deck under the real suit/copy structure. Rearrangement alone cannot satisfy an emptying goal.\n- Size sink capacity and parallelism to the deck\u2019s true completion structure, not to convention. Duplicated suits, repeated suit-runs, reduced-suit decks, or multiple deck copies often require different foundation counts or capacities than a standard 52-card template.\n- Match the sink rule to the deck and to ordinary play. Prefer sinks that accept progress in small, common steps; all-at-once completion gates or sinks that only fit rare preassembled patterns usually create churn instead of wins.\n- When a one-way receiving or accumulation area already exists, usually make it the main victory sink, expand it if needed, or align the win condition to it before adding new subsystems or more tableau freedom.\n- One-way piles must either represent rewarded completion or have a reliable release route. Otherwise they become dead storage, blockers, or mathematically unwinnable bookkeeping.\n- Keep main progress sinks genuinely sink-like: irreversible by default, with specific entry conditions when needed to prevent them from becoming generic parking or accidental churn routes.\n- Make sure every participating pile type has a path into the objective. If a stock, deal pile, or auxiliary area feeds play but is omitted from completion logic, the game may be structurally unwinnable.\n\n## Shape mobility to support progress\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Near-zero use, 0\u20133 move losses, and tiny exhausted searches usually indicate missing opening contact or no reachable first progress route; high move counts, broad usage, and search-cap losses usually indicate reversible motion without enough rewarded progress.\n- When movement is plentiful but wins are absent, improve the sink before adding mobility: verify capacity, align victory to real progress, and make ordinary exposed cards able to score irreversible advancement.\n- When wins already exist but losses mostly hit search limits or solutions are very long, tighten the broadest reversible routes before adding new ones. Unrestricted empty-space fills, loose helper piles, and generic low-commitment shuffling are common churn sources.\n- Favor movement rules that organize cards in the same direction the sink rewards. Stack moves or tableau builds are best when they convert exposure into sink-ready order rather than free circulation disconnected from victory.\n- Broad holding areas are risky. Helper piles should be clearly temporary and clearable, or else be converted into structured progress zones that directly support completion.\n\n## Tune openings and workspace with small changes\n- For frequent 0- or 1-move losses in an otherwise coherent game, first improve opening contact with the smallest compatible change: slightly more starting exposure, a narrowly available workspace destination, or a modest increase in effective tableau capacity.\n- Prefer constrained empty-pile rules over unrestricted ones. Rank-gated, single-card, or otherwise narrow access to empties often rescues dead starts while preserving planning value and reducing solver noise.\n- Keep empty-space permissions internally consistent. If empty columns are intended as workspace, single-card and stack movement should be able to use them under compatible conditions; mismatched access rules create accidental dead ends and needless churn.\n- If some deals already win cleanly but others die almost immediately, suspect blocked opening information or cramped early workspace before rewriting the core build rules.\n- When a stock or deal pile feeds cards into active play, use count-preserving setup changes that preserve that flow where possible; pacing elements work best when they expose or supply cards that can realistically reach the main sink.\n```\n\nThis generation produced 100 insight(s) from independently revised variants:\n\n1. When a receiving pile can accept almost any card with no sequencing constraint, it stops being a progress sink and becomes dead-simple storage; wins may stay high, but decisions and pile relevance collapse. Tightening the sink to reward ordered progress often improves the game more than changing tableau mobility.\n2. When a one-way sink builds by suit from aces, its number of parallel starts must match the deck\u2019s actual number of suit-sequences, not a standard template. If the sink cannot mathematically absorb the whole deck, extra mobility usually creates search churn rather than real solvability.\n3. When losses exhaust in 0\u20134 moves across all shuffles, the problem is usually structural, not tactical: check whether the victory condition has a real irreversible sink that can absorb the whole deck under the actual suit/copy structure. In reduced-suit games, standard ace-only foundation starts can accidentally cap progress far below the deck size unless the number and role of sink piles are aligned to that smaller deck.\n4. When a game\u2019s win condition requires active piles to empty, first check that its irreversible sink has enough total capacity for the actual deck composition. Multi-copy or reduced-suit decks often need more foundation piles than a standard template, and without that capacity the game is mathematically unwinnable no matter how much tableau mobility you add.\n5. When a solitaire already has lots of legal movement but most losses hit search limits, the bottleneck is often the completion sink, not tableau mobility. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, relaxing sink matching from exact suit to a broader criterion can turn ordinary exposure into real irreversible progress without rewriting the whole game.\n6. When a game already has a valid sink and some wins, but many losses burn through large search budgets with broad card usage, the problem is often reversible single-card churn rather than lack of access. Adding a constrained stack-move rule can increase meaningful decision-making while reducing pointless rearrangement, as long as the stack pattern matches the direction of eventual progress.\n7. When a solitaire file fails to compile because card totals are off, fix the arithmetic before changing gameplay. Count-preserving repairs that keep the existing pile roles intact are the best first step, because they restore meaningful playtest feedback without confounding it with a redesign.\n8. When a solitaire uses duplicated decks or repeated suits, standard-looking foundation counts can make the game mathematically unwinnable even if movement feels rich. Before tuning mobility, make sure the irreversible sink has the right parallel capacity for the deck\u2019s actual completion structure.\n9. When most losses are fully exhausted after only a handful of moves, the first thing to fix is usually opening contact, not late-game scoring. In small-deck solitaire especially, hidden cards can turn otherwise-sound movement rules into mathematically dead starts; increasing initial exposure is often the smallest effective repair.\n10. When a solitaire shows high move counts with no wins, first check whether its one-way sinks can actually absorb the full deck under normal play. Small dead-end storage piles create search churn; converting them into incremental completion piles aligned with the deck\u2019s structure usually helps more than adding extra tableau freedom.\n11. When a game already wins every shuffle with near-total card usage, the problem is often not lack of content but overly permissive reversible movement. Tightening the broadest workspace rule\u2014while leaving the main sink intact\u2014can add real decision-making without breaking the game\u2019s core flow.\n12. When a solitaire has plenty of legal movement but never wins, check whether the victory sink can actually absorb the deck under its real multiplicities. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, foundation count often needs to scale with duplicate runs, not with familiar four-suit conventions.\n13. When a solitaire file fails deck accounting, prefer a minimal count-preserving repair that keeps the existing move graph intact; only after the game compiles and every card has a path into the win condition can difficulty tuning mean anything.\n\nIf you introduce a new source pile purely to restore missing cards, make sure it participates in both movement and victory. Otherwise you trade a compile failure for a structurally unwinnable design.\n14. When a solver reaches deep search caps with broad card and pile usage but never wins, the problem is often not lack of movement but lack of accessible irreversible progress. In reduced-suit or duplicated-deck designs, sink rules should usually match the deck\u2019s real granularity (such as color groups rather than exact suits) so ordinary exposed cards can advance completion instead of only enabling more tableau churn.\n15. When a variant fails before simulation, the best next step is usually a minimal accounting repair that preserves the intended card flow; you learn much more from a runnable but imperfect game than from a larger speculative redesign layered on top of a compile error.\n16. When many losses are fully exhausted despite substantial legal movement, first check whether the game lacks enough single-card workspace to expose sinkable cards. A narrow relaxation on empty-pile access for single cards can rescue blocked openings without creating the full churn that comes from also relaxing stack placement.\n17. When a solitaire explores deeply with high pile usage but never wins, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of meaningful irreversibility. Converting one auxiliary area into a true one-way completion sink, and aligning the win condition to it, usually improves solvability more than adding yet more movement.\n18. When a solitaire has plenty of legal movement but never wins, first check whether the completion sink can actually absorb the deck under the real suit/copy structure. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, foundation count and foundation entry rules often need to be sized to the true number of suit sequences, not inherited from standard single-deck conventions.\n19. When a solitaire already wins sometimes but most losses are long, non-exhaustive searches with high card usage, the problem is often excessive reversible mobility rather than lack of access. Tightening the broadest empty-pile moves is a good first way to reduce churn without breaking the core sink structure.\n20. When a variant\u2019s win condition is \u201cempty the active piles,\u201d it still needs an irreversible sink that can absorb the whole deck under the actual deck structure. If a pile type only accepts cards one-way, give it clear completion logic and align victory to the piles that can realistically be emptied.\n21. When some deals already complete while many others are proven dead after very short searches, the bottleneck is often opening contact rather than endgame capacity. A small increase in starting exposure can improve fairness across shuffles more cleanly than broadening movement rules or adding new pile types.\n22. When many losses are immediate or near-immediate, improve opening contact before changing the scoring sink: adding a few more active piles or reducing per-pile congestion can create meaningful first moves without greatly increasing churn.\n\nIf a deal stock distributes one card to every tableau pile, the number of tableau piles also controls stock pacing and workspace density; changing pile count can fix both dead openings and awkward deal rhythm at once.\n23. When a solitaire\u2019s win condition depends on emptying active piles, first check whether the completion sink has enough parallel capacity for the deck\u2019s actual number of suit-runs or build sequences. If the sink is undersized, no amount of extra mobility will fix the game; players will only churn among reversible moves.\n24. When a solitaire asks active piles to empty, first verify that the completion piles can absorb the entire deck under the actual suit and copy count. Extra \"standard\" foundations are not harmless decoration; mismatching sink capacity to deck structure can make a game mathematically unwinnable even if local move rules seem fine.\n25. When some deals win fully but many others fail in 0\u201315 moves, the core objective may already be sound; the more likely issue is opening contact. A very small increase in starting exposure, paired with a count-preserving reduction elsewhere, can improve solvability without flattening the game into triviality.\n26. When a solitaire asks multiple working areas to empty, its irreversible receiving piles must be able to absorb the deck through common, repeatable moves\u2014not just occasional special matches. If a thematic sink rule is too narrow to carry the whole win condition, keep the theme but add a second, ordinary completion route instead of only loosening tableau mobility.\n27. When most losses are fully exhausted and happen almost immediately, check whether the win condition is mathematically reachable before adding more mobility. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, foundation count and starting occupancy must match the deck\u2019s real completion structure, or the game becomes impossible no matter how much tableau movement exists.\n28. When a game\u2019s win condition requires active piles to empty, first verify that the sink can mathematically absorb the entire deck under the actual deck composition. In reduced-suit or nonstandard decks, standard \u201cAce-up only\u201d foundations may leave most cards with no completion path at all; a bidirectional or otherwise broader sink can restore solvability more effectively than adding more tableau mobility.\n29. When a solitaire shows high move counts and broad pile/card usage but no wins, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but lack of a common irreversible sink. In duplicated or reduced-suit decks, relaxing sink requirements to match the real card distribution often improves solvability more than adding more tableau movement.\n30. When a solitaire repeatedly hits search limits with moderate-to-high card usage but no wins, the problem is often reversible single-card churn rather than lack of access. In that situation, adding a structured stack move that preserves the same ordering required by the sink can convert busy motion into real progress without rewriting the whole game.\n31. When a reduced-suit deck uses suit-matched ascending foundations, the number of foundation piles should usually match the number of suit sequences the deck can actually complete. Overprovisioned sink piles can signal a deeper mismatch between deck structure, card accounting, and victory flow.\n32. When a deal-type stock is the only reliable way to populate an initially empty active pile, gating the deal on \u201call active piles non-empty\u201d can create immediate unwinnable starts. Before tuning difficulty, ensure the opening flow can actually establish contact between all core piles.\n33. When a game asks the player to empty all working piles, first verify that its irreversible sinks can actually absorb the whole deck under the real deck multiplicity. In multi-deck games, \u201cstandard\u201d foundation counts often create endless churn rather than true difficulty.\n34. When a solitaire file fails to compile, prefer the smallest repair that restores a runnable core while preserving pile roles and card flow; only after that should you tune difficulty or decision depth. Also verify that win conditions only reference pile types allowed in that rules context, since draw/stock piles are often governed separately from ordinary pile-type checks.\n35. When a win condition requires the tableau to empty, first check that the game has enough true sink capacity for the actual deck composition. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, using the \u201cstandard\u201d number of foundations can make the game mathematically unwinnable even if play feels active.\n36. When a solitaire fails basic deck accounting, fix that with the smallest change that preserves the existing pile roles and card flow; getting to a runnable version is more valuable than speculative difficulty tuning. If missing cards are restored via a stock or draw source, make sure those cards can reach the same progress sinks as the rest of the layout so the repair is informative rather than introducing a new dead zone.\n37. When a design hasn\u2019t reached simulation yet, fix card accounting with the smallest count-preserving change that keeps the existing flow intact; you learn more from a runnable near-neighbor than from a speculative redesign. In multi-deck or reduced-suit games, re-check sink capacity separately from total card count: a valid setup can still hide a mathematically impossible win structure.\n38. When a solitaire has plenty of reversible motion but no wins, first check whether the existing \u201cfoundation\u201d or sink can actually absorb the whole deck under its entry rule. If not, repurpose or retarget an existing pile type into an irreversible completion sink that accepts common, naturally-built patterns rather than adding yet more mobility.\n39. If the win condition is \u201cempty the tableau,\u201d the game needs an irreversible sink that ordinary play can actually feed on most deals. When losses are fully exhausted after only a few moves, fixing sink reachability usually matters more than adding more tableau mobility.\n40. When most losses are fully exhausted after only a handful of moves, the problem is often blocked opening information rather than insufficient rule complexity. In that situation, increasing initial exposure is a cleaner first repair than loosening every movement rule, because it tests whether the existing progress structure is fundamentally sound.\n41. When a solitaire already generates lots of legal motion but never finishes, suspect a progress-sink mismatch before adding more freedom. In multi-copy reduced-suit decks, rewarding full ordered runs as irreversible completion often produces cleaner progress than forcing every card through standard ace-up foundations.\n42. When a rules engine restricts which pile types a win clause may reference, fix the objective by leaning on equivalent board-state goals already implied by card flow instead of rewriting the whole game. In single-deck builders, \u201call active piles empty\u201d plus fully-sized foundations often captures stock exhaustion indirectly and preserves the original design intent with minimal disruption.\n43. When a solitaire has lots of legal rearrangement but no wins, first check whether its irreversible sink matches the deck\u2019s real completion units. In repeated-suit or multi-copy decks, replacing a narrow ace-up foundation with a sink that accepts whole natural runs can convert churn into genuine progress without rewriting the tableau.\n44. When a game already has an irreversible sink, make the win condition track actual completion rather than unrelated occupancy patterns in auxiliary piles. Also, if many losses show near-zero card usage or immediate exhaustion, revealing more initial information can rescue the opening without changing the core move graph.\n45. When a game wins consistently because one auxiliary pile accepts everything with no restriction, the issue is often not raw win rate but loss of structure: unrestricted storage erases meaningful tradeoffs. Turning such a pile into constrained workspace, and giving it a clear route back into the main progress system, can preserve solvability while increasing decision value.\n46. When a solver explores deeply without winning, the problem is often not lack of mobility but lack of alignment between mobility and scoring. Make the main rearrangement rule build toward the sink's acceptance pattern, so common moves create irreversible progress instead of reversible churn.\n47. If a win requires active areas to empty, the game needs enough irreversible sink capacity to absorb the full deck in small, common increments. Narrow \u201cpairing\u201d sinks or a single generic receiving pile often create mathematically unwinnable designs even when legal moves exist.\n48. When a solitaire already wins on many deals but losses mostly end at search limits with high card/pile usage, the main problem is often reversible churn rather than lack of access. In that situation, tightening the broadest empty-space move\u2014especially stack-to-empty moves\u2014can improve decision quality without removing the workspace function entirely.\n49. When a game already has a valid sink but many deals fail in 0\u20133 moves, the bottleneck is often opening information rather than overall mobility. Increasing starting exposure is a small, high-signal change that can rescue dead starts without fundamentally changing the game\u2019s core flow.\n50. When a solitaire already wins often but solutions are long and occasional losses come from deep, non-exhaustive searches, reduce the cheapest reversible move first rather than adding power. Unrestricted moves into empty piles are a common source of solver churn; rank-gating empties can preserve planning value while making progress routes more committal.\n51. When a solitaire file fails to compile, prefer the smallest rule-language fix that preserves the current card flow and objective. You need a runnable baseline before difficulty or balance signals from simulation mean anything.\n52. When a game already wins every shuffle and some tableau piles are underused, unrestricted access to empty spaces is often the simplest source of triviality. Gating empty fills by rank can keep the same overall structure while turning spare columns from generic parking into a real planning resource.\n53. If a solitaire asks active piles to empty, it needs an irreversible sink that can absorb the whole deck in realistic increments. Auxiliary piles that only hold cards without a completion structure create either dead starts or endless rearrangement, not solvable progress.\n54. When a game uses duplicated suits or multiple deck copies, foundation count must match the number of distinct full suit-runs the deck contains. If the win requires tableau piles to empty, a sink that can absorb only part of the deck creates endless churn rather than meaningful difficulty.\n55. When a variant already wins often and losing runs fail by hitting search limits rather than by exhausting legal play, the problem is usually excessive reversible state space, not lack of mobility. In those cases, trimming the most repeatable source of churn\u2014such as unlimited stock redeals\u2014can improve practical playability without rewriting the core build rules.\n56. When a solitaire\u2019s win condition requires all active piles to empty, first verify that the irreversible sink has enough total capacity for the actual deck composition. Multi-copy or reduced-suit decks often need more foundation piles than a standard layout, and no amount of tableau freedom can compensate for an undersized sink.\n57. When a game shows high move counts and broad pile usage but no wins, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but an overly narrow irreversible sink. In multi-deck games especially, slightly broadening foundation compatibility can convert churn into genuine progress without rewriting the whole tableau.\n58. When losses come from long, non-exhaustive searches rather than immediate dead ends, first examine whether the stock rhythm is creating churn or hiding needed access. Changing draw cadence can improve both solvability and decision quality without rewriting the tableau or sink structure.\n59. When losses show high move counts and broad pile usage but zero wins, suspect churn before deadness: the layout is active, but progress is not being rewarded irreversibly enough to reach the win.\nFor multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, completion piles should usually be able to keep building from their own top cards; if only fresh cards can enter a sink, its capacity may exist on paper but be unreachable in play.\n60. When a solitaire\u2019s win condition requires most play piles to empty, first verify that the receiving sink has enough parallel capacity to absorb the full deck under the actual deck composition. A game with lively movement but no wins often needs a mathematical sink-capacity fix before it needs more mobility.\n61. When a solitaire explores many states but never wins, first check whether ordinary exposed cards can make irreversible progress toward the win. A sink that only accepts rare, fully assembled patterns often creates endless reshuffling; replacing it with small-step scoring moves usually improves both solvability and decision quality more than adding extra mobility.\n62. When a win condition requires tableau piles to empty, first verify that the game has enough one-way sink capacity for the actual deck composition. Duplicated suits or multiple deck copies often require more foundation piles even if the move rules themselves are otherwise sound.\n63. When a pile type accepts cards but does not itself represent completion, it usually needs a reliable release path; otherwise it becomes dead storage that inflates search without improving solvability.\n\nIf losses consistently hit search limits with high pile/card usage, prefer fixing one-way helper piles or sink alignment before broadly loosening all movement rules.\n64. When a solitaire asks the player to empty all active piles, first verify that the irreversible sink can absorb the entire deck under the actual suit/copy structure. If the sink\u2019s total capacity is too small, adding more movement only creates search churn rather than real progress.\n65. When a design hasn't even reached simulation yet, preserve as much of the intended move graph as possible and fix the smallest structural error first. Exact card accounting is not bookkeeping trivia in solitaire design; a one-card mismatch can hide all the real gameplay problems behind a compile failure.\n66. When a solitaire already has some wins but many losses end at search limits with high move counts, prefer tightening the noisiest reversible move rather than adding more freedom. In particular, limiting stack-transfer size can preserve meaningful combo play while sharply reducing churn from generic bulk shuffling.\n67. When a solitaire already has some wins but many losses consume the search budget, look for the broadest reversible move and make it match the game\u2019s actual progress structure. Tightening stack movement to the same constraints as single-card building often preserves meaningful planning while cutting unproductive churn.\n68. When some deals already win but many losses are long, non-exhaustive searches with high pile/card usage, the problem is often excessive reversible mobility rather than insufficient access. A small restriction on the broadest empty-pile moves can improve decision quality and reduce churn without dismantling the game\u2019s core flow.\n69. When a solitaire has lots of legal motion but no wins, first check whether its supposed sink can actually absorb the whole deck under the real suit/copy count. Empty-the-tableau win conditions need enough foundation parallelism and capacity to match duplicated suits and multiple deck copies; otherwise the game produces search-heavy churn instead of progress.\n70. When a solitaire fails basic deck accounting, fix that before tuning difficulty: the most informative repair is usually to restore the missing cards inside existing active pile roles rather than adding new subsystems. Count-preserving setup fixes should keep the original move graph intact so later playtest results reflect gameplay, not a hidden structural rewrite.\n71. If a game requires tableau piles to empty, it needs enough irreversible sink capacity to absorb the whole deck under the actual deck composition. A one-way pile that accepts cards too rarely\u2014or only once\u2014creates either immediate impossibility or endless rearrangement with no real progress.\n72. When a game asks the player to empty all active piles, first check whether the completion area has enough parallel capacity for the deck\u2019s true structure. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, a \u201cstandard\u201d number of foundations can silently make the game unwinnable even if movement rules are generous.\n73. When a solitaire has lots of legal rearrangement but no wins, the usual problem is not lack of mobility but lack of an irreversible sink that matches how the tableau naturally organizes cards. In multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, prefer completion moves that reward the patterns players already build (like full descending runs) over standard ace-up foundations that may be technically valid but rarely reachable in practice.\n74. When a game\u2019s objective is to empty active piles, first verify there is enough irreversible sink capacity to absorb the whole deck under the actual deck composition. Multi-copy or reduced-suit decks often need more completion piles than a standard template, and without that capacity even a playable tableau becomes mathematically unwinnable.\n75. If a game asks active piles to empty, it needs an irreversible destination that can absorb the whole deck in ordinary increments. A one-way pile that is excluded from the victory structure, or a \u201csink\u201d that only accepts rare patterns, often makes the layout mathematically unwinnable even when plenty of local moves exist.\n76. When a game has a mathematically valid win sink but all losses are fully exhausted with near-zero usage, the usual problem is opening contact, not endgame capacity. In that situation, a small relaxation of tableau building rules often helps more than adding new pile types or changing the victory condition.\n77. When a solitaire already wins sometimes but many losses hit search limits with high move counts, the problem is often excessive reversible workspace rather than insufficient mobility. Tightening the broadest empty-space permission is a good first adjustment because it reduces churn without removing the core progress route.\n78. When a rules engine allows only one move definition per source/destination pair, merging exceptions into a single condition tree is usually better than inventing extra pile types or rerouting card flow. First restore a compilable, behaviorally close ruleset; only then use playtest data to judge whether the merged rule is too strong or too weak.\n79. When a solitaire already wins on some shuffles but other deals are proven impossible, a small increase in starting information can be better than adding broader movement rules. More initial exposure often fixes structural dead starts without turning the tableau into unrestricted churn.\n80. When a ruleset is unwinnable by structure, fix the completion path before tuning difficulty: every active card must have a legal route into a sink or into the win condition. Also, when a special draw pile isn\u2019t a normal pile type in the language, align victory checks to the pile predicates the engine actually allows rather than referencing it directly.\n81. When a solitaire asks the tableau to empty, first check whether its one-way sink can actually absorb the whole deck under the deck\u2019s real suit structure. If progress piles are too numerous, too few, or built for the wrong completion pattern, the result is often a mix of immediately impossible deals and long reversible churn.\n82. When a solitaire file fails to compile, fix the arithmetic and structural validity before tuning difficulty; even a strong rules idea is untestable if the deck isn\u2019t fully accounted for.\nA minimal count-preserving repair is usually best first, because it lets later simulation data reveal whether the underlying move and win structure actually works.\n83. When a game shows high move counts and broad pile usage but no wins, first check whether the irreversible sink can actually absorb the full deck under the real suit/copy structure. In multi-deck or duplicated-suit variants, using too few foundation piles often creates pure churn: cards can be rearranged extensively, but ordinary progress still has nowhere permanent to go.\n84. When a variant has no simulation data because it fails to compile, the best next step is usually the smallest count-preserving repair that restores the intended card flow. You learn more from first making the current structure runnable than from redesigning rules before the setup is even testable.\n85. When some deals already win but many losses terminate in just a few fully-explored moves, prefer a small increase in starting information or exposure before loosening the core move rules. Early visibility often fixes dead openings without turning the rest of the game into unrestricted churn.\n86. When a solitaire file fails to compile from card-count mismatch, fix that before tuning difficulty; preserving the existing pile roles and correcting the stock count is often the least disruptive way to recover a testable design.\n\nFor multi-copy or reduced-suit decks, re-sum the entire setup explicitly after every count change; \u201calmost right\u201d stock sizes can silently remove whole deal rounds and make later balance conclusions meaningless.\n87. When losses are exhausted almost immediately, first check for a structural win-path failure before tuning difficulty: if the only sink cannot absorb the deck under the actual suit/copy setup, no amount of tableau mobility will help.\n\nFor reduced- or duplicated-suit decks, foundation count should match how many independent suit sequences the deck contains, not the number used in a standard 52-card template.\n88. When a solitaire file fails to compile, fix the rule-context error as locally as possible before tuning gameplay. In particular, win conditions and move conditions often have similar-looking predicates, but they are not interchangeable; preserving the existing pile roles while repairing the context mismatch gives cleaner evaluation data for the next round.\n89. When some deals win fully but others are proven unwinnable after only a few moves, the bottleneck is often opening contact rather than overall difficulty. A small increase in starting information or exposure on the most cramped piles can improve fairness without flattening the game\u2019s midgame.\n90. When a solitaire file fails basic deck accounting, fix that before tuning difficulty: a minimal count-preserving repair that keeps existing pile roles intact gives you meaningful playtest data next round without confounding the design.\n91. When a solitaire\u2019s win condition requires active piles to empty, first check that its sink piles can absorb the entire deck under the actual deck composition. Duplicate suits or multiple deck copies often require more parallel sink capacity even if the move rules themselves look reasonable.\n92. When a variant fails before playtesting, fix arithmetic and structural validity first with the smallest count-preserving change you can. Early tuning of difficulty or strategy is meaningless until every card has a legal place in the setup and the intended move graph can actually run.\n93. When losses are fully exhausted after 0\u20133 moves, suspect a structural mismatch before tuning difficulty: the deck, sink capacity, and opening mobility may simply not support a path to victory. In reduced-suit or repeated-suit decks, foundation count and tableau permissions often need to be recalibrated together; fixing only one usually leaves the game either unwinnable or inert.\n94. When losses mostly hit search limits with high card usage, suspect excessive reversible mobility rather than insufficient access. Tightening the broadest empty-space rule is often a better next step than adding more movement, especially if wins already exist.\n95. When many deals are proven unwinnable after only a handful of moves, the problem is often opening workspace rather than overall difficulty. A small relaxation on how empty piles can be used can rescue dead starts while preserving the main build and scoring structure.\n96. When a game asks all active piles to empty, first check whether its irreversible sink can actually absorb the whole deck under the real deck structure. If not, expanding mobility won't help; the sink role or the win condition must be realigned before difficulty tuning means anything.\n97. When a solitaire file fails basic deck accounting, fix that with the smallest count-preserving change that preserves pile roles and flow before tuning difficulty. A runnable but imperfect design gives useful playtest signals; an uncompilable one gives none.\n98. When a solitaire shows broad pile usage and long non-exhaustive losses, the problem is often reversible churn rather than lack of legal moves; in those cases, add movement that preserves and rewards meaningful structure before adding more generic workspace.\n\nIf the victory sink builds from exposed order one card at a time, tableau rules should help assemble that order efficiently; otherwise the game can have many moves without real progress.\n99. When a design has plenty of legal motion but no wins, first check whether the irreversible sink can actually absorb all active cards required by the win condition. Expanding or repurposing an existing one-way receiving pile into a true completion sink is often more effective than adding still more mobility.\n100. When a solitaire repeatedly hits search limits with moderate-to-high pile usage but no wins, the problem is often not lack of movement but a sink that is too narrow for the deck\u2019s duplication structure. Before loosening tableau rules further, make sure the irreversible completion area has enough parallel capacity to absorb the full deck in ordinary-sized steps.\n\nProduce the next version of skill.md: rewrite it as a whole, integrating what's genuinely new or well-supported from the insights above into the existing document rather than appending to it. Keep the entire document to at most 20 bullet points total, organized under a small number of clear headers. End your response with the complete new skill.md in a single fenced code block tagged `skill`, as the last thing in your response."}, {"role": "assistant", "content": "```skill\n# skill.md\n\n## Establish a valid, playable core\n- Treat compile failures, bad deck accounting, invalid references, impossible predicates, rule-slot conflicts, and vacuous win clauses as first-order design failures. Restore a runnable game before tuning difficulty.\n- Prefer minimal, informative repairs: preserve pile roles, card flow, move graph, and exact card totals where possible. A runnable near-neighbor reveals far more than a speculative redesign layered on top of a broken file.\n- Re-check rule/setup compatibility after every structural fix. Nonstandard deck composition, duplicated suits, multiple deck copies, and engine-specific rule contexts can make familiar-looking move or win rules invalid, unreachable, or misleading.\n- If missing cards are restored or new piles are introduced during repair, make sure those cards still participate in both movement and victory; otherwise you replace a compile problem with a structurally unwinnable game.\n- Keep move definitions compact and non-overlapping. If the engine effectively allows one rule per source/destination pair, merge exceptions into one coherent condition tree instead of splitting behavior across competing moves.\n\n## Align sinks, victory, and deck structure\n- If the win condition requires active piles to empty, the design needs a reachable, irreversible sink that can absorb the entire deck under the actual suit/copy structure. Rearrangement alone is not a completion plan.\n- Size sink capacity and parallelism to the deck\u2019s true completion units, not to standard 52-card conventions. Reduced-suit, duplicated-suit, and multi-copy decks often need different numbers of foundations or different sink structures entirely.\n- Match sink entry rules to ordinary play. Prefer sinks that accept progress in small, common increments; sinks that only fit rare preassembled patterns, only accept fresh entries, or only score in all-at-once bursts usually create churn instead of wins.\n- When movement is plentiful but wins are absent, fix sink mismatch before adding mobility. Broadening sink compatibility, changing the rewarded completion pattern, or repurposing an existing one-way pile into the main victory sink is usually more effective than making the tableau looser.\n- Keep one-way piles purposeful. If a receiving area is not itself a rewarded completion sink, give it a reliable release route; otherwise it becomes dead storage or search noise.\n- Avoid overly permissive sinks. A pile that accepts almost anything without meaningful sequencing stops being a progress sink and becomes generic storage, which can preserve win rate while collapsing decisions and pile roles.\n- Align the win condition to actual completion. Track the piles that represent irreversible progress, and make sure every participating pile type has a legal path into that objective.\n\n## Shape mobility to support progress\n- Distinguish deadness from churn. Very short exhausted losses usually indicate blocked opening contact or structural impossibility; long high-usage losses and search-cap failures usually indicate reversible motion without enough rewarded progress.\n- Favor movement rules that build in the same direction the sink rewards. Tableau and stack moves are most useful when they convert exposure into sink-ready order instead of supporting free circulation disconnected from victory.\n- When wins already exist but many losses are long, tighten the broadest reversible routes before adding new freedom. Unrestricted empty fills, oversized stack transfers, unlimited redeals, and loose helper piles are common churn sources.\n- Prefer constrained workspace over unrestricted parking. Rank-gated empties, single-card access, or stack rules that mirror ordinary building usually preserve planning value while reducing solver noise.\n- If mobility is high but progress is shallow, consider adding or tightening structured stack moves rather than more generic single-card freedom. Movement that preserves meaningful order can reduce churn while improving real decision-making.\n\n## Tune openings and pacing with small changes\n- When losses are exhausted in 0\u20134 moves, first check for a real win path; if the sink is valid, then treat the problem as opening contact. Small increases in starting exposure often rescue dead starts more cleanly than broadening all movement rules.\n- Use narrow workspace fixes before broad mobility buffs. A little more initial information, a modest increase in tableau capacity, or limited single-card access to empties can unblock openings without flattening the game.\n- If some deals already win but many others die immediately, suspect cramped starts more than flawed endgame structure. Improve fairness across shuffles with the smallest exposure or workspace change that preserves the core flow.\n- When changing tableau count or stock/deal behavior, remember that pile count also controls pacing, congestion, and how quickly cards enter active play. Count-preserving setup changes can fix both opening contact and deal rhythm at once.\n```"}]